+## Subtopics
+
+### Gradient-Based Shading Interpolation
+This variant interpolates between surface-shaded and unshaded results using the gradient magnitude to prevent homogeneous regions from being surface shaded [S2]. The interpolation factor uses the formula $1 - (1 - ||\nabla f||)^2$ to weight the surface shading contribution, allowing midrange gradient magnitudes to be interpreted as surface features [S2]. The final result is modulated by light attenuation (shadows) regardless of the surface shading state [S2].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel for the lighting and blending step, requiring 6 neighbor lookups for gradient estimation and constant-time transfer function access [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory per voxel for the current step, plus storage for the transfer functions and volume data [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** The method is computationally lightweight per voxel, suitable for CPU-based direct volume rendering as indicated by the source repository [S1, unofficial]. The 6 neighbor lookups and transfer function accesses add overhead compared to simple alpha blending [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Provides surface-like shading effects without explicit surface extraction and is straightforward to implement [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Gradient estimation via central differences can be noisy in low-resolution volumes, and boundary clamping may distort normals at edges [S1, unofficial]. The method assumes a specific memory layout and transfer function format [S1, unofficial].
